---
import InsightArticle from '../../layouts/InsightArticle.astro';
import ColumnChart from '../../components/insights/ColumnChart.astro';
import LogBarChart from '../../components/insights/LogBarChart.astro';
import DataNote from '../../components/insights/DataNote.astro';
import insights from '../../data/insights/2026-11-14.json';
import check from '../../data/insights/2026-11-14.openrouter-check.json';

const rawBase = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const base = rawBase.endsWith('/') ? rawBase : `${rawBase}/`;

const spread = insights.priceSpread;
const all = spread.all; // sorted cheapest to dearest by output price
const cheapest = all[0];
const dearest = all[all.length - 1];
const usd = (v: number) => (v < 1 ? `$${v.toFixed(2)}` : `$${Number.isInteger(v) ? v : v.toFixed(2)}`);
const isReseller = (source: string) => /openrouter/i.test(source);
const snapDate = new Date(insights.snapshotDate).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' });

// All of these are computed by scripts/insights/compute-insights.mjs.
const ladder = spread.ladder;
const labCheapest = spread.labListCheapest;
const labDearest = spread.labListDearest;
const labRatio = Math.round(spread.labListRatio);
const labRows = spread.rangeByLab;
const live = check.outputPrices;
const checkDate = new Date(check.checkedAt).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' });
const liveDearest = live.dearest.filter((m) => m.outputPerMillion === live.dearest[0].outputPerMillion);
const liveName = (id: string) => all.find((m) => id.endsWith(`/${m.id}`))?.name ?? id;

const under1 = spread.bands[0].count;
const over50 = spread.bands[spread.bands.length - 1].count;
---

<InsightArticle
  title="The dearest AI model costs 6,000 times more than the cheapest"
  description="Across 65 active language models we track, the price of a million output tokens runs from $0.03 to $180. Where the models sit in between, why the two ends are not like for like, and what the gap does not prove."
  date="2026-11-14"
  type="Analysis"
  readTime="5 min"
  snapshot={insights.snapshotDate} standfirst="A million tokens of output costs three cents from the cheapest model we track and $180 from the dearest. Both are for sale today. The gap is real, but the two ends are not selling the same thing."
>
  <p>
    {cheapest.name} will write you a million tokens for {usd(cheapest.output)}. {dearest.name} charges {usd(dearest.output)} for the same amount. On OpenAI's <a href="https://help.openai.com/en/articles/4936856-what-are-tokens-and-how-to-count-them" rel="noopener">rule of thumb</a> that 100 tokens is about 75 words, a million tokens is roughly 750,000 words. So the same pile of text costs three cents
    or $180 depending on which model writes it.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is a ratio of {Math.round(spread.ratio).toLocaleString('en-GB')} to one, across the {spread.n} language models we
    list as active and priced on {snapDate}.
  </p>

  <div class="stat-row">
    <div class="stat"><b>{usd(cheapest.output)}</b><span>cheapest, per million output tokens ({cheapest.name})</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>{usd(dearest.output)}</b><span>dearest, per million output tokens ({dearest.name})</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>{Math.round(spread.ratio).toLocaleString('en-GB')}x</b><span>dearest divided by cheapest</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>{usd(spread.medianOutput)}</b><span>median output price across {spread.n} models</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>The ladder</h2>
  <p>
    Sort the {spread.n} models by output price and take every fifth one, and you get the ladder below. The scale is
    logarithmic, which means each gridline is ten times the one before. On an ordinary scale everything under $10 would be
    squashed flat against the left edge.
  </p>

  <LogBarChart
    title="Output price per million tokens, every fifth model from cheapest to dearest"
    summary={`Output prices run from ${usd(cheapest.output)} for ${cheapest.name} to ${usd(dearest.output)} for ${dearest.name}.`} ticks={[0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 100, 1000]} data={ladder.map((m, i) => ({
      label: m.name,
      value: m.output,
      valueLabel: usd(m.output),
      highlight: i === 0 || i === ladder.length - 1,
    }))} caption={`Source: AI Resource Hub catalogue, snapshot of ${snapDate}. US dollars per million output tokens. Logarithmic scale. Prices under $1 for third-party hosted models are the cheapest host listed by OpenRouter.`}
  />

  <p>
    The median output price is {usd(spread.medianOutput)} per million
    tokens and the median input price is {usd(spread.medianInput)}.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the models sit</h2>

  <ColumnChart
    title="Active language models by output price band"
    summary={`Models per price band: ${spread.bands.map((b) => `${b.label}: ${b.count}`).join('; ')}.`} data={spread.bands.map((b) => ({ label: b.label, value: b.count, valueLabel: String(b.count), highlight: b.label.startsWith('$50') }))} caption={`Source: AI Resource Hub catalogue, snapshot of ${snapDate}. Count of models out of ${spread.n}. Bands are US dollars per million output tokens.`}
  />

  <p>
    The spread is fairly even until the top. {under1} of the {spread.n} models charge under a dollar per million output
    tokens. Only {over50} charge $50 or more: five "Pro" tiers from OpenAI and Anthropic's Claude Fable 5.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption>Source: AI Resource Hub catalogue, snapshot of {snapDate}. US dollars per million tokens.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Five cheapest</th><th class="num">Input</th><th class="num">Output</th><th>Price from</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {spread.cheapest.slice(0, 5).map((m) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{m.name} <span style="color:var(--color-text-muted)">({m.provider})</span></td>
            <td class="num">{usd(m.input)}</td>
            <td class="num">{usd(m.output)}</td>
            <td>{isReseller(m.source) ? 'Cheapest host on OpenRouter' : "Lab's own list"}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Five dearest</th><th class="num">Input</th><th class="num">Output</th><th>Price from</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {spread.dearest.slice(0, 5).map((m) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{m.name} <span style="color:var(--color-text-muted)">({m.provider})</span></td>
            <td class="num">{usd(m.input)}</td>
            <td class="num">{usd(m.output)}</td>
            <td>{isReseller(m.source) ? 'OpenRouter listing' : "Lab's own list"}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>The two ends are not like for like</h2>
  <p>
    Look at the "price from" column. The cheap end is made up of small models such as Mistral Nemo, Llama 3.1 8B and Phi 4
    that anyone may host, priced at whichever host on OpenRouter is cheapest that day. The dear end is a lab's own list price
    for its top tier. Comparing them is a bit like comparing the cheapest supermarket's own-brand with a restaurant's tasting
    menu. Both are food and the price gap is true, but nobody thinks they are the same product.
  </p>
  <p>
    A fairer test is to use only prices taken from the labs' own pricing pages. We hold {spread.fromLabList} of those among the
    active models. They run from {labCheapest.name} at {usd(labCheapest.output)} to {labDearest.name} at {usd(labDearest.output)}, which is {labRatio} to one. Both of those are OpenAI models, so one lab's own price list spans
    more than a hundredfold.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption>
        Source: AI Resource Hub catalogue, snapshot of {snapDate}. Labs with at least three active, priced language models.
        US dollars per million output tokens. Cheapest figures may be a third-party host's price.
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Lab</th><th class="num">Models</th><th class="num">Cheapest</th><th class="num">Dearest</th><th class="num">Range</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {labRows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{r.lab}</td>
            <td class="num">{r.n}</td>
            <td class="num">{usd(r.min)}</td>
            <td class="num">{usd(r.max)}</td>
            <td class="num">{Math.round(r.max / r.min).toLocaleString('en-GB')}x</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Anthropic stands out in that table for a different reason. Its cheapest model in this set, Claude Haiku 4.5, costs $5 per million output tokens,
    which is more than the cheapest from every other lab listed.
  </p>

  <h2>What the gap does not prove</h2>
  <p>
    It would be neat to finish by saying whether the $180 model is 6,000 times better. We cannot say, and we would be wary of
    anyone who claims to. Price per token is easy to measure. Quality is not, and we do not yet hold independent test results
    we are allowed to publish alongside these prices. Our <a href={`${base}transparency/`}>transparency page</a> explains what
    is missing and why.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is a second gap in this comparison. Price per token is not cost per job. A model that reasons at length before it
    answers uses more output tokens to do the same task. OpenAI's <a href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/reasoning" rel="noopener">documentation</a> (read on 14 November 2026) says reasoning tokens "are billed as output tokens" and can run to tens of thousands per
    response. So a higher price can be multiplied by a higher token count. Two
    models with the same list price can produce very different bills.
  </p>

  <h2>Start at the cheap end</h2>
  <p>
    If the job is simple and the volume is high,
    such as sorting messages into categories, pulling fields out of documents or writing short summaries, try a model from the
    bottom third of the ladder first and move up only if the results are not good enough. Trying the cheap model on a small sample
    first costs very little, and you will know from the output whether you need to pay more.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the sums on your own workload, use the <a href={`${base}calculator/`}>cost calculator</a>. The <a href={`${base}guides/understanding-ai-pricing/`}>guide to AI pricing</a> covers input, output and cached tokens, and
    the <a href={`${base}cheapest/`}>cheapest models page</a> keeps a current list.
  </p>

  <DataNote anchor="calc-price-spread" snapshot={`${snapDate} (catalogue rebuilt ${insights.cacheGeneratedAt.slice(11, 16)} UTC)`}>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>What we counted.</strong> {spread.n} language models that we mark as active and that have an input and an
        output price above zero. Free models and entries removed by the variant filter (ids ending in a date stamp or "-fast") are left out. Newer models whose prices we are still
        confirming are marked "tracking" and are not in this count.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Checked against the source.</strong> On {checkDate} we read OpenRouter's live public list of {check.listings} entries. Its cheapest paid model was {liveName(live.cheapest[0].id)} at {usd(live.cheapest[0].outputPerMillion)} and its dearest {liveDearest.length > 1 ? 'were' : 'was'} {liveDearest.map((m) => liveName(m.id)).sort().reverse().join(' and ')} at {usd(live.dearest[0].outputPerMillion)}, the same two ends as our catalogue. The result is saved with this article's
        data.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Two kinds of price.</strong> {spread.fromReseller} of the {spread.n} prices come
        from OpenRouter, which reports the cheapest host for each model. {spread.fromLabList} come from a lab's own pricing page
        and were typed in by hand. The table shows which is which.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>List prices only.</strong> No batch discounts, cached-token discounts or volume deals are included.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </DataNote>
</InsightArticle>
